# Untitled

Trimetallic Metal-Organic Framework Nanoframe Superstructures: A Stress-Buffering Architecture Engineering of Anode Material toward Boosted Lithium Storage Performance
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) can serve as prevailing anodes for lithium-ion batteries, due to their multiple redox-active sites and prominent structural compatibility. However, the poor electronic conductivity and inferior cyclability hinder their further implementation. Herein, a synthetic methodology for trimetallic Fe-Co-Ni MOFs with nanoframe superstructures architecture (Fe-Co-Ni NFSs) via structural evolution is proposed for versatile anode materials for lithium storage. Ascribed to optimal compositional and structural optimization, the Fe-Co-Ni NFSs achieve exceptional electrochemical performance with superior specific capacity (1030 mAh g\(^{-1}\) at 0.1 A g\(^{-1}\)), outstanding rate capacity (414 mAh g\(^{-1}\) at 2 A g\(^{-1}\)), and prolonged cyclability (489 mAh g\(^{-1}\) upon 1000 cycles at 1 A g\(^{-1}\)). Both experimental and theoretical investigations reveal that the multi-component metal centers could boost electronic conductivity, confer multiple active sites, and energetically favor Li adsorption capability. Additionally, the nanoframe superstructures of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs could facilitate stress-buffering effect on volumetric expansion and prevent electrode pulverization, further improving the lithium storage capability. This work envisions a meticulous protocol for high-performance MOF anode materials for lithium-ion batteries.
## 1. Introduction
Lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) have emerged as the most prevailing energy storage systems (ESSs) due to their superior power density and ultralong cycling life, which are dependent upon the progress and development in anode technology.[1,2] However, the limited theoretical capacity of commercialized graphite (372 mAh g\(^{-1}\)) and lithium titanate (175 mAh g\(^{-1}\)) anodes encounters limitations in lithium storage applications.[3,4] Adhered to the cultivation-superior-anodes-oriented strategy, considerable studies have been focused on addressing the bottlenecks including restricted specific capacity, sluggish reaction kinetics, and unsatisfactory lifespan.[5]
Among the multitude of alternatives, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), coordinated by metal nodes and organic ligands to construct porous crystalline structure, have been prevailing in anode materials for lithium storage recently.[6,7] Although MOFs have prevalently served as the self-sacrificial templates to synthesize metal oxides, phosphides, selenides, sulfides, and carbonaceous anodes via high-temperature calcination, the advantages of MOFs may be weakened in the derivatives and the synthesis strategies are diverse and complicated, further inducing high manufacturing costs.[8,9] Instead, pristine MOFs have been popularized as the versatile LIB anodes.[10] Ascribing to the sufficient interpenetrating tunnels, porous crystalline structures, ordered active sites, and controllable morphology, MOFs are theoretically conducive to alleviate volume expansion, mitigate electrode pulverization, and shorten diffusion distance, contributing to boosted transport kinetics and superior electrochemical performance.[11,12] Particularly, the discharge capacity can be further improved by the N-containing functional ligands owing to the guest/host interactions among the N and Li atoms in reversible storage mechanism.[13] Interestingly, MOFs with elastic chemical coordinative bonds are deemed to effectively alleviate the volume contraction and expansion during lithiation/delithiation.[14] Prussian blue (PB) and Prussian blue analogs (PBAs), as one kind of MOFs with 3D open cubic structure, are denoted by the chemical formula \(\mathrm{A_{x}M_{1}[M_{2}M_{3}(C N)_{6}]_{1-y}\cdot\gamma\cdot z H_{2}O}\) (A = alkali metal ions, M = Ni, Co,
fides, and carbonaceous anodes via high-temperature calcination, the advantages of MOFs may be weakened in the derivatives and the synthesis strategies are diverse and complicated, further inducing high manufacturing costs.[8,9] Instead, pristine MOFs have been popularized as the versatile LIB anodes.[10] Ascribing to the sufficient interpenetrating tunnels, porous crystalline structures, ordered active sites, and controllable morphology, MOFs are theoretically conducive to alleviate volume expansion, mitigate electrode pulverization, and shorten diffusion distance, contributing to boosted transport kinetics and superior electrochemical performance.[11,12] Particularly, the discharge capacity can be further improved by the N-containing functional ligands owing to the guest/host interactions among the N and Li atoms in reversible storage mechanism.[13] Interestingly, MOFs with elastic chemical coordinative bonds are deemed to effectively alleviate the volume contraction and expansion during lithiation/delithiation.[14] Prussian blue (PB) and Prussian blue analogs (PBAs), as one kind of MOFs with 3D open cubic structure, are denoted by the chemical formula \(\mathrm{A_{x}M_{1}[M_{2}M_{3}(C N)_{6}]_{1-y}\cdot\gamma\cdot z H_{2}O}\) (A = alkali metal ions, M = Ni, Co,
Fe, Mn, etc., \(\square\) represents vacancies, and the numerical value of x, y, and z relates to the stoichiometry).[15] Benefiting from the high electrochemical activity and multiple chemical valences of the transition metal ions, PBAs are inclined to store numerous charge to achieve superior lithium storage capacity.[16] The large cavities generated by the hexacyanometalate framework accommodate lithium ions and the 3D open frameworks enable fast diffusion kinetics.[17] Unfortunately, PBAs have been rarely reported as anodes for lithium storage, especially their rarely explored storage mechanism.[18]
PBAs have suffered from their intrinsically low electronic conductivity and structural instability as (de)lithiation proceeds.[19] It is imperative to achieve the structural and compositional optimization for PBAs. To surmount the electronic conductivity, introduction of multiple metallic components in PBAs is conducive to generate local defects and electronic delocalization with a local built-in electric field.[20,21] Therefore, 3D channel structure of PBAs could guarantee that the metal nodes enable the electronic localization by synergetic interaction, which could effectively facilitate dynamic mobility and boosted electronic conductivity. However, PBAs with bulk morphology prepared via the conventional coprecipitation strategy suffer from sluggish ion diffusion due to the underutilized active sites.[22] Therefore, the functionality and structural hierarchy of PBAs are intriguing, when 3D hierarchical architectures are rationally constructed by converting the bulk PBAs into hollow frames with open walls. This guarantees the sufficient contact between electrode/electrolyte and provides extra active sites for lithium storage.[23,24] To the best of our knowledge, the trimetallic PBAs with 3D hierarchically hollow frame superstructure have not been utilized as LIB anodes to ameliorate electronic conductivity and improve electrochemical performance until now.
Herein, we propose a self-templating synthetic strategy for trimetallic Fe-Co-Ni MOFs with nanoframe superstructures architecture (FeCo-Ni NFSs) as high-performance anode for LIBs. Systematic investigations from experiments to theoretical calculation unveil that the trimetallic component and nanoframe superstructure of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs synchronously facilitate the boosted electronic conductivity, extra active sites, and mitigated volume variation upon (de)lithiation for excellent lithium storage performance. Benefiting from the structural and compositional superiorities, the Fe-Co-Ni NFSs anode achieves superior electrochemical performance with exceptional specific capacity (1030 mAh \(\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(0.1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}\)), outstanding rate capacity \((414\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}\) at \(2\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}\)), and ultralong cyclability \((489\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}\) upon 1000 cycles at \(1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}\)).
## 2. Results and Discussion
The self-templating synthetic strategy for Fe-Co-Ni NFSs is schematically illustrated in Figure 1a, which includes template constructing and framework etching procedures. Firstly, the trimetallic Fe-Co-Ni nanocubes (NCs) as MOF-template are self-assembled via solution coprecipitation method. Afterward, the etching manipulation enables the nanocubes architecture to evolve into nanoboxes (NBs), nanoframes (NFs), and nanoframe superstructures (NFSs) by optimal prolongation of solvothermal duration. The panoramic structural evolution by etching of Fe-Co-Ni MOFs was morphologically and structurally investigated by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM). As depicted in Figure 2a,b, the Fe-Co-Ni NCs feature in a nanocubic geometry with relatively smooth surface, illustrating a bulk morphology. As for the self-templating etching
process, the morphologic configuration of the material transforms from nanocube (Fe-Co-Ni NCs) to carved nanobox (Fe-Co-Ni NBs), and then into nanoframe (Fe-Co-Ni NFs) architectures upon etching prolongation,[25] which inherit the morphological characteristics of Fe-Co-Ni NCs prototype but holds rougher surface (Figure 2c–f). In the FeCo-Ni NFs, the interior cavities and the 3D open framework are conducive to alleviated volume fluctuations, exceptional charge diffusivity, and enriched defective surface sites, further enriching the electrochemical kinetics.[26] Finally, the sophisticated 3D-hollow nanoframe superstructures (NFSs) constructed by nanosize building blocks with open walls are observed (Figure \(2\mathrm{g-i}\)). It uncovers the hierarchical open nature of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs by pronounced contrast within the exterior pillar and interior void, facilitating the primary omnibearing stress-release points to buffer the diffusion-induced strains.[27,28] The high-resolution TEM image (Figure 2j) reveals that the well-resolved lattice fringes with interplanar d-spacings of \(0.52\,\mathrm{nm}\) corresponds to the (200) lattice facet of \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), manifesting that the \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{III})\) in trimetallic Fe-Co-Ni NFSs has been coordinated with \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{II})\) without crystalline phase transformation. The corresponding fast Fourier transform (FFT) and selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern image confirm that the Fe-Co-Ni NFSs bears single crystalline configuration (Figure 2k,l). High-angle annular dark-field scanning TEM (HAADF-STEM) and element mappings demonstrate the homogeneous elemental distributions of Fe, Co, Ni, C, N, and O elements of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs (Figure \(2\mathrm{m}\)), indicating the successful construction of trimetallic FeCo-Ni NFSs.
The crystallographic configuration for Fe-Co-Ni NCs, Fe-Co-Ni NBs, Fe-Co-Ni NFs, and Fe-Co-Ni NFSs were identified by X-ray diffraction (XRD) spectroscopies. According to Figure 3a, the typical diffraction peaks at \(17.3^{\circ}\), \(24.6^{\circ}\), \(35.1^{\circ}\), and \(39.4^{\circ}\) are designated to the (200), (220), (400), and (420) facets of cubic \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) phase (JCPDS No. 82-2283) with \(\mathrm{F}\overline{{4}}3\mathrm{m}\) space group, indicating the formation of pure face-centered-cubic trimetallic PBA with high crystallinity (Figure S1, Supporting Information). The chemical structures of all the as-prepared samples were investigated by Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra (Figure 3b). The presence of adsorbed and interstitial water is indicated by the vibration peaks at 3648 and \(3410~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) for \(\mathrm{O-H}\) stretching band and \(1618~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) for \(\mathrm{O-H}\) bending band.[29] A cyanide stretching vibration band and cyanide bending modes at approximately 2184 and \(593~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) are related to the \(\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}{-}\mathrm{C}\equiv\mathrm{N}{-}\mathrm{Ni}^{\mathrm{II}}\) ionic-covalent bond,[30] whereas the correlative characteristic peaks located at 2104 and \(453~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) coincide with \(\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{III}}{-}\mathrm{C}\equiv\mathrm{N}{-}\mathrm{Ni}^{\mathrm{II}}\) ionic-covalent bond.[31] Raman spectra depicted in Figure 3c show that the peaks positioned at 2120 and \(2184~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) are assignable to \(\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{III}}{-}\mathrm{C}\equiv\mathrm{N}{-}\mathrm{Ni}^{\mathrm{II}}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}{-}\mathrm{C}\equiv\mathrm{N}{-}\mathrm{Ni}^{\mathrm{II}}\), respectively.[32] The pronounced peak intensity of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs infers the optimized crystallinity upon etching conditions. The bonding states and chemical compositions were elucidated by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The survey-scan XPS spectra in Figure 3d confirm the coexistence of Fe, Co, Ni, C, N, and O elements in Fe-Co-Ni NFSs, in which the O element may attributed to water species in the framework. The Fe 2p core-level spectra were deconvoluted into two characteristic subpeaks of 708.9 and \(722.1\,\mathrm{eV}\) associated with Fe(III) \(2{\tt p}_{3/2}\) and Fe(III) \(2{\tt p}_{1/2}\) in Fe-Co-Ni NFSs (Figure 3e).[33] The peaks at 780.6 and \(796.4\,\mathrm{eV}\) in the high-resolution Co 2p spectra were split into spin-orbitals of Co(III) \(2{\tt p}_{3/2}\) and Co(III) \(2{\tt p}_{1/2}\) (Figure 3f). Figure 3g illustrates that the Ni 2p species with fitted peaks of 856.3 and \(873.9\,\mathrm{eV}\) belong to \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{II})\) \(2{\tt p}_{3/2}\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{II})\) \(2{\tt p}_{1/2}\), respectively.[34] Meanwhile, the thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA) results reveal that mass loss below \(200\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) can be
credited with the elimination of physical adsorbed water (from room temperature to \(100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)) and interstitial water (from \(100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to \(200\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)) molecules (Figure S2, Supporting Information), which indicates the superior thermal stability for practical application in electrode materials.[35] To confirm the element ratios and chemical structure of all samples, inductively coupled plasma (ICP) analysis, elemental analysis, and TGA were concurrently used. According to these results, the chemical formula for Fe-Co-Ni NCs, Fe-Co-Ni NBs, Fe-Co-Ni NFs, and Fe-Co-Ni NFSs can be represented as \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}[\mathrm{Fe}_{0.51}\mathrm{Co}_{0.53}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}\cdot7.73\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\) \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}[\mathrm{Fe}_{0.54}\mathrm{Co}_{0.49}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}\cdot7.13\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\) \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}[\mathrm{Fe}_{0.48}\mathrm{Co}_{0.52}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}\cdot6.35\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}[\mathrm{Fe}_{0.51}\mathrm{Co}_{0.46}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}\cdot6.56\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\) respectively. Ascribing to the high porosity and 3D open framework of MOF, the porous nature of the as-prepared material was studied by \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) absorption–desorption isotherms at \(77\,\mathrm{~K~}\) (Figure 3h). The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs takes up to \(86.5\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\), which is higher than that of Fe-Co-Ni NCs \((22.8\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})\), Fe-Co-Ni NBs \((38.8\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})\), and Fe-Co-Ni NFs \((55.6\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})\). Additionally, the pore-size distribution ranges from 1 to \(55\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 3i), revealing the interior open channels. The hierarchical micro-, meso-, and macro-porous structure of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs guarantees shorter diffusion pathways, more exposed active sites, and improved pseudocapacitive contribution, further boosting the diffusion kinetics and comprehensive performance. In brief, FeCo-Ni NFSs feature an intriguing open framework configuration from exterior nanoscale morphology to interior crystallographic nature.
To elucidate the structural and compositional integrity of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs, the lithium storage properties of all the samples were comprehensively investigated. Figure 4a presents the initial three cyclic
To elucidate the structural and compositional integrity of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs, the lithium storage properties of all the samples were comprehensively investigated. Figure 4a presents the initial three cyclic voltammetry (CV) profiles of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs. An obvious irreversible reduction peak at \(\sim\!1.28\mathrm{~V~}\) in the initial cathodic scan can be ascribed to the electrolyte decomposition and solid-electrolyte interphase (SEI) formation.[36] A broad cathodic peak positioned at \(0.49\mathrm{~V~}\) is associated with the reduction of \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{III})\) to \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{III})\) and \(\mathrm{Co}(0)\) together with reduction of \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{II})\) to \(\mathrm{Ni}(0)\). Then, two peaks emerge at around 0.87 and \(0.43\mathrm{~V~}\) in the following cycles owing the structural reconstruction of active material after initial lithiation.[37] Meanwhile, the reduction peak appearing at \(0.13\mathrm{~V~}\) is assignable to the transformation from \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{III})\) into \(\mathrm{Fe}(0)\). In the oxidative process, the broad anodic peak at around \(1.48\mathrm{~V~}\) corresponds to the conversions from \(\mathrm{Fe}(0)\) to \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{III})\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}(0)\) to \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{II})\).[38,39] Similarly, owing to the oxidation of low valence state Co into \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{III})\), the broad peak positioned at \(1.95\mathrm{~V~}\) can be detected. This hypothesis of lithium storage mechanism will be discussed later. The superimposition of the CV profiles from the second cycle onward manifests the superior reversibility of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs. It is significant that the CV of Fe-Co-Ni NCs, Fe-Co-Ni NBs, Fe-Co-Ni NFs do not appreciably differentiate from that of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs (Figure S3, Supporting Information), indicating that the structural evolution exerts little influence on the conversion reaction. The corresponding characteristic peaks of Fe-Co-Ni MOFs are similar to the electrochemical reaction of Fe-Ni MOFs and Co-Ni MOFs (Figure S4, Supporting Information), respectively. Figure 4b records representative galvanostatic charge/discharge (GCD) profiles of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs at \(0.4\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}\), in which the (de)lithiation behavior coincides with the aforementioned CV analysis, and the holistic CE of the Fe-Co-Ni NFSs electrode quickly stabilizes at \(\sim\!100\%\) as the (de)lithiation proceeds. The Fe-Co-Ni NFSs exhibit the impressive cycling performance with a capacity of \(1030\ \mathrm{mAh\ g^{-1}}\) at \(0.1\ \mathrm{A\ g^{-1}}\) after 120 cycles (Figure 4c) and \(783\ \mathrm{mAh\ g^{-1}}\) at \(0.2\ \mathrm{A\ g^{-1}}\) upon 600 cycles (Figure 4e). However, the Fe-Co-Ni NCs, Fe-Co-Ni NBs, and Fe-Co-Ni NFs anodes display only the inferior capacity of \(219\ \mathrm{A\ g^{-1}}\), and \(606\ \mathrm{mAh\ g^{-1}}\) at \(0.2\ \mathrm{A\ g^{-1}}\) cycling to 600 cycles, respectively, and lag far behind that of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs, which may be ascribed to the mechanical degradation induced by the serious volume variation and sluggish reaction kinetics due to the long diffusion pathways. Additionally, the dual-metallic Fe-Ni MOFs, Co-Ni MOFs, and Fe-Co MOFs show inferior electrochemical performance (Figure S5, Supporting Information). As shown in Figure 4e, the specific capacity fading of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs electrodes during initial several cycles may be ascribed to the unstable formation of SEI film and structural degradation of electrode materials.[40,41] Figure 4d illustrates the rate performance of Fe-Co-Ni NCs, Fe-Co-Ni NBs, Fe-Co-Ni NFs, and Fe-Co-Ni NFSs anodes at low \((0.04\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1})\) to ultrahigh \((2\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1})\) current densities. As expected, the Fe-Co-Ni NFSs demonstrate a superiority of rate capability and outperform those of their counterparts, delivering reversible rate capacities of 1222, 1013, 863, 729, 569, and \(414\ \mathrm{mAh\ g^{-1}}\) at 0.04, 0.1, 0.2, 0.4, 1, and \(2\ \mathrm{A\ g^{-1}}\),
corresponding characteristic peaks of Fe-Co-Ni MOFs are similar to the electrochemical reaction of Fe-Ni MOFs and Co-Ni MOFs (Figure S4, Supporting Information), respectively. Figure 4b records representative galvanostatic charge/discharge (GCD) profiles of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs at \(0.4\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}\), in which the (de)lithiation behavior coincides with the aforementioned CV analysis, and the holistic CE of the Fe-Co-Ni NFSs electrode quickly stabilizes at \(\sim\!100\%\) as the (de)lithiation proceeds. The Fe-Co-Ni NFSs exhibit the impressive cycling performance with a capacity of \(1030\ \mathrm{mAh\ g^{-1}}\) at \(0.1\ \mathrm{A\ g^{-1}}\) after 120 cycles (Figure 4c) and \(783\ \mathrm{mAh\ g^{-1}}\) at \(0.2\ \mathrm{A\ g^{-1}}\) upon 600 cycles (Figure 4e). However, the Fe-Co-Ni NCs, Fe-Co-Ni NBs, and Fe-Co-Ni NFs anodes display only the inferior capacity of \(219\ \mathrm{A\ g^{-1}}\), and \(606\ \mathrm{mAh\ g^{-1}}\) at \(0.2\ \mathrm{A\ g^{-1}}\) cycling to 600 cycles, respectively, and lag far behind that of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs, which may be ascribed to the mechanical degradation induced by the serious volume variation and sluggish reaction kinetics due to the long diffusion pathways. Additionally, the dual-metallic Fe-Ni MOFs, Co-Ni MOFs, and Fe-Co MOFs show inferior electrochemical performance (Figure S5, Supporting Information). As shown in Figure 4e, the specific capacity fading of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs electrodes during initial several cycles may be ascribed to the unstable formation of SEI film and structural degradation of electrode materials.[40,41] Figure 4d illustrates the rate performance of Fe-Co-Ni NCs, Fe-Co-Ni NBs, Fe-Co-Ni NFs, and Fe-Co-Ni NFSs anodes at low \((0.04\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1})\) to ultrahigh \((2\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1})\) current densities. As expected, the Fe-Co-Ni NFSs demonstrate a superiority of rate capability and outperform those of their counterparts, delivering reversible rate capacities of 1222, 1013, 863, 729, 569, and \(414\ \mathrm{mAh\ g^{-1}}\) at 0.04, 0.1, 0.2, 0.4, 1, and \(2\ \mathrm{A\ g^{-1}}\),
respectively, whereas the Fe-Co-Ni NCs just deliver \(75~\mathrm{mAh~g^{-1}}\) at \(2\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}\). When the current density rebounds to \(0.04\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}\), the capacity maintains at \(1095\,\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}\), endowing the high reversibility upon (de)lithiation. Herein, the trimetallic active sites, 3D open framework, and nanobuilding blocks facilitate the boosted electrochemical conductivity and improved intercalation pseudocapacitance, further contributing to fast charge diffusivity. Moreover, due to the coordinated water in the structure would be irreversibly removed during the (dis)charge process, the less coordinated water of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs in the structure facilitates higher specific capacity and superior rate performance.[42,43]
The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) spectra were recorded after the first deep cycle, as illustrated in Figure 4f. All the Nyquist plots are coupled with a low-frequency inclined line pertaining to the Warburg impedance \(\left(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{w}}\right)\), and a high-to-medium frequency
semicircle equivalent to the interfacial charge transfer resistance \(\left(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}\right)\). Clearly, the low-frequency inclined line of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs features much steeper slope, elucidating the boosted diffusivity of lithium ions into the active materials. Moreover, the Fe-Co-Ni NFSs achieve much lower \(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}\) than that of other counterparts, which the hierarchical open framework with fabulous internal cavity facilitates charges to diffuse faster from the interior and exterior of the nanoframe superstructures and the diffusion from external surface to internal bulks can be avoided, thus effectively reducing \(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}\). To further evaluate the cycling stability of the Fe-Co-Ni NFSs, the long-term cyclability under high current density was recorded in Figure \(4\mathrm{g}\). As expected, Fe-Co-Ni NFSs electrode delivers an ideal \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\)storage capacity of \(489\,\mathrm{mAh\,g^{-1}}\) even at \(1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}\) after 1000 loops. Benefiting from the fabulous structure and composition optimization, the rate performance and cycling stability of Fe-Co-Ni
NFSs exceed than other recently reported MOF-based anodes in LIBs, as shown in Table S1 (Supporting Information). To verify practicability and feasibility of Fe-Co-Ni NFs for practical application, the full-cell performance with Fe-Co-Ni NFs as anode and commercial Prussian Blue (PB, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{4}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{3}\)) as cathode was assembled and investigated (Figure S6a, Supporting Information), demonstrating the feasibility of all PB/PBAs-based lithium-ion batteries. The electrochemical performance of commercial PB is depicted in Figure S7 (Supporting Information). The Fe-Co-Ni \(\mathrm{NFSs}/\mathrm{PB}\) full-cell achieves a retained discharge capacity of \(155.8\ \mathrm{mAh\ g^{-1}}\) at \(0.1\ \mathrm{A\ g^{-1}}\) upon 150 cycles, together with a CE of \(\sim\!100\%\) (Figure S6b, Supporting Information), revealing the superior cycling performance. It is noteworthy that the electrochemical performance of the Fe-Co-Ni \(\mathrm{NFSs}/\mathrm{PB}\) full-cell may be further focused on optimizing the ratio and type of other suitable cathode with Fe-Co-Ni NFs anode.
To expound a profound insight into lithium-ions storage mechanism of Fe-Co-Ni NFs, in-depth ex-situ analysis was comprehensively
investigated by XPS, and XRD techniques. Figure S8 (Supporting Information) shows the XPS survey spectra of the Fe-Co-Ni NFs upon different (de)lithiation states. Further proved by the Li 1s core-level spectra (Figure S9a, Supporting Information), the existence of Li at all the selected states demonstrates that the lithium ions have intercalated into the channels of Fe-Co-Ni NFs lattice for charge balance. Additionally, the C 1s spectra illustrate the presence of cyanide bonds during (de) lithiation (Figure S9b, Supporting Information). As depicted in Figure 5a, the characteristic peaks at 708.7 and \(721.5\ \mathrm{eV}\) of core-level Fe 2p spectra correspond to the Fe(III) \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and Fe(III) \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) upon lithiation to \(0.91\ \mathrm{V}\).[44,45] Whereas, another component with characteristic peaks at 705.6 and \(716.9\ \mathrm{eV}\) for Fe(0) \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and Fe(0) \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) emerges upon discharging to \(0.35\ \mathrm{V}\) and then charging to \(1.24\ \mathrm{V}\) but disappears upon charging back to 1.60 and \(3\ \mathrm{V}\), indicating that the descent of the oxidation state of Fe by the reduction of low-spin Fe(III) during cycling. The Co 2p spectra can be deconvoluted into two components pertaining to Co(II) (782.4 and 797.7 eV for Co \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and Co \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\))
and \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{III})\) (780.9 and \(796.2\ \mathrm{eV}\) for Co \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and Co \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\)) upon lithiation to \(0.91\ \mathrm{V}\).[46,47] Beyond that, extra peaks pertaining to metallic cobalt appears at \(777.6\) and \(791.9\ \mathrm{eV}\) for Co \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and Co \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) when lithiation to \(0.35\ \mathrm{V}\) and then delithiation to \(1.60\ \mathrm{V}\), showing that the consecutive reduction of low-spin \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{II})\) to \(\mathrm{Co}(0)\) (Figure 5b).[48] However, the characteristic peaks of \(\mathrm{Co}(0)\) decrease upon full delithiation, correlating to the oxidation of \(\mathrm{Co}(0)\) into higher chemical valence. In terms of the high-spin Ni component, the coexistence of \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{II})\) (856.1 and \(873.7\ \mathrm{eV}\) for Ni \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and Ni \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\)) and \(\mathrm{Ni}(0)\) (852.2 and \(870.1\ \mathrm{eV}\) for Ni \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and Ni \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\)) can be observed after lithiation to \(0.35\ \mathrm{V}\) and the subsequent delithiation process, demonstrating the reduction of high-spin \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{II})\) to \(\mathrm{Ni}(0)\) (Figure 5c).[49] It is of note that all the metallic Ni, Co, and Fe components are synchronously conducive to the boosted electronic conductivity and accelerated electrochemical diffusion. More precisely, the multi-component of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs poses synergistic effect at the different voltage ranges and further exalts lithium-ion storage performance.
Ex-situ XRD patterns of different (de)lithiation states upon initial cycles was investigated to uncover the crystalline evolution of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs, where the characteristic diffraction signals at around 43.1, 50.2, 73.9, and \(89.6^{\circ}\) are assigned to the electrode current collectors of
Ex-situ XRD patterns of different (de)lithiation states upon initial cycles was investigated to uncover the crystalline evolution of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs, where the characteristic diffraction signals at around 43.1, 50.2, 73.9, and \(89.6^{\circ}\) are assigned to the electrode current collectors of copper foil. As illustrated in Figure S10a (Supporting Information), the crystallographic configuration of pristine Russian blue analogous along with peaks at \(\sim\!17.1\), 24.3, 34.9, and \(39.1^{\circ}\) is still maintained as discharging to \(1.2\ \mathrm{V}\), indicating that the \(\mathrm{Li}^{+}\)insertion does not destroy the crystal structure of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs. However, it gradually vanishes as lithiation to \(0.75\ \mathrm{V}\), correlating to the conversion of Fe, Co, and Ni components; and it does not recover to its initial status after full delithiation (Figure S10b, Supporting Information), demonstrating that an irreversible crystalline to amorphous phase transition occurred during the initial (de)lithiation process. Moreover, both the metallic Fe, Co, and Ni phase are not detected by XRD, which can be ascribed to the dispersion of Fe, Co, and Ni inside the amorphous matrix. It is common in metal oxides that the metal particles are well-dispersed in \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) matrix upon lithiation.[18,50] The amorphous materials may be conducive to the exalted cyclability due to the isotropic property of the amorphous matrix, which facilitates homogenous volume variations and buffers tremendous volume strain.[51] Hence, the in-depth insights of the electrochemical reaction mechanism can be elucidated as follow:
\[
\mathrm{Ni}_3^{\mathrm{II}}\left[\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}^{\mathrm{III}}\mathrm{Co}_{0.5}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{CN})_6\right]_2+\mathrm{Li}^++\mathrm{e}^-\leftrightarrow\mathrm{LiNi}_3^{\mathrm{II}}\left[\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}^{\mathrm{III}}\mathrm{Co}_{0.5}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{CN})_6\right]_2
\]
\[
\begin{array}{rl}\mathrm{LiNi}_3^{\mathrm{II}}\big[\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}^{\mathrm{III}}\mathrm{Co}_{0.5}^{\mathrm{II}}\big(\mathrm{CN}\big)_6\big]_2&+8\mathrm{Li}^++8\mathrm{e}^-\leftrightarrow3\mathrm{Ni}^0+\mathrm{Co}^0\\&+\mathrm{Li}_3\big[\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{CN})_6\big]+6\mathrm{LiCN}\end{array}
\]
\[
\mathrm{Li}_3\left[\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{CN})_6\right]+3\mathrm{Li}^++3\mathrm{e}^-\leftrightarrow\mathrm{Fe}^0+6\mathrm{LiCN}
\]
To elucidate the intriguing intercalation pseudocapacitive storage of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs, CV profiles with scan rates from 0.2 to \(1\ \mathrm{mV\ s^{-1}}\) were comprehensively conducted (Figure 5d). The total capacity of anode materials includes diffusion contribution induced by conversion reactions and pseudocapacitive contribution caused by surface charge transfer. The capacitive-controlled kinetics numerically obey the equation as follows:[52]
\[
\mathrm{i}=\mathrm{a}^{\mathrm{b}}
\]
Generally, a \(b\)-value approaching 0.5 correlates with a diffusion-dominant process, while a \(b\)-value approaching 1 relates to a
capacitive-dominant kinetics. The corresponding \(b\)-values (0.852 for peak I and 0.771 for peak II, Figure 5e) of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs are higher than those of Fe-Co-Ni NCs (0.722 for peak I and 0.74 for peak II, Figure S11b, Supporting Information), confirming that capacitive contribution is dominant in Fe-Co-Ni NFSs. The pronounced increase of \(b\)-values of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs disclose that hierarchical nanoframe superstructures boost electrochemical kinetics compared with the bulk structure. Quantitatively, the capacitive contribution can be evaluated by the following laws:[52]
\[
\mathrm{i}(V)=\mathrm{k}_1\nu+\mathrm{k}_2\nu^{1/2}
\]
where \(\mathrm{k}_{1}\nu\) and \(\mathrm{k}_{2}\nu^{1/2}\) correspond to the pseudocapacitive- and diffusion-controlled processes. Herein, a dominating pseudocapacitive contribution of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs takes up to \(65.9\%\) at \(0.8\ \mathrm{mV\ s^{-1}}\), which is superior than that of Fe-Co-Ni NCs (53.7%), according to Figures S11c and S12 (Supporting
Information). The pseudocapacitive contribution increases upon the increasing scan rate, whereas the pseudocapacitive ratio of Fe-Co-Ni NCs is smaller than that of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs under similar conditions (Figure 5f; Figure S11d, Supporting Information), demonstrating that the from-crystal-to-architecture dual-level open framework provides considerably shorter diffusion pathway and extra active sites for pseudocapacitive storage.
Galvanostatic intermittent titration technique (GITT) was employed to further numerically analyze the kinetics of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) diffusion coefficient \(\left(\mathrm{D_{Li}}^{+}\right)\)upon (de)lithiation. Figure S13 (Supporting Information) depicts the voltage response of Fe-Co-Ni NCs, Fe-Co-Ni NBs, Fe-Co-Ni NFSs, and Fe-Co-Ni NFSs electrodes. The slight potential change during every relaxation period is correlative to the overpotential for the corresponding (de)lithiation period (Figure S14, Supporting Information).[53] Clearly, the diffusion in all four trimetallic MOFs holds the same tendency, inferring the analogous \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) storage mechanism. The \(\mathrm{D_{Li}}^{+}\)values during (de)lithiation process of Fe-Co-Ni NCs, Fe-Co-Ni NBs, Fe-Co-Ni NFSs, and Fe-Co-Ni NFSs electrodes are illustrated, displaying a pronounced change at \(\mathrm{~\bar{~}{~0.75~V~}~}\) in the lithiation process (Figure \(5\mathrm{g}\) ) and \(0.9{-}2\,\mathrm{~V~}\) during the delithiation process (Figure 5h), where the minimal \(\mathrm{D_{Li}}^{+}\)coincides with the phase transformation kinetics in these potentials and leads to sluggish kinetics performance.[54] The \(\mathrm{D_{Li}}^{+}\)values of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs vary from \(1.27\times10^{-10}\) to \(3.58\,\times\,10^{-8}\,\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\,\,\mathrm{S}^{-1}\) during the discharging processes and from \(1.80\,\times\,10^{-10}\) to \(4.78\,\times\,10^{-8}\,\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\,\,\mathrm{S}^{-1}\) during charging processes. More importantly, the Fe-Co-Ni NFSs electrode bears higher \(\mathrm{D_{Li}}^{+}\)values than those of Fe-Co-Ni NCs, Fe-Co-Ni NBs, and Fe-Co-Ni NFs, showing superior electrochemical kinetics alongside the 3D open nanoframe superstructures owing to the overall diffusion path, rather than diffusion-limited kinetics inside the solid bulk material. Otherwise, the \(\mathrm{D_{Li}}^{+}\)can be obtained by the EIS results using the following relationship:[55]
\[
\mathrm{D_{Li}}^{+}=\mathrm{R}^{2}\mathrm{T}^{2}/2\mathrm{A}^{2}\mathrm{n}^{4}\mathrm{F}^{4}\mathrm{c}^{2}\mathfrak{sigma}^{2}
\]
where the \(\upsigma\) (Warburg coefficient) is obtained by the slope of \(Z^{\prime}\) - \(\omega^{-1/2}\) plots at low frequency. The \(\mathrm{D_{Li}}^{+}\)is in inverse relationship to the \(\upsigma\) , hence, the Fe-Co-Ni NFSs shows the lowest \(\upsigma\) \((25.1\ \Omega\ \mathrm{S}^{-1})\) but the highest \(\mathrm{D_{Li}}^{+}\) (Figure 5i) than that of Fe-Co-Ni NFs \((43.2\ \Omega\ \mathrm{S}^{-1})\) , Fe-Co-Ni NBs \((59.8\ \Omega\ \mathrm{S}^{-1})\) , and Fe-Co-Ni NCs \((61.8\ \Omega\ \mathrm{S}^{-1})\) . This result also demonstrates that the Fe-Co-Ni NFSs holds the fastest diffusion rate, which is conforms well with the GITT analysis.
The structural integrity of nanoframe superstructured Fe-Co-Ni MOFs is further investigated by ex-situ SEM during reversible cycling. Upon different cycling status, the structure of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs was well-maintained upon different cycling states (Figure S15a–c, Supporting Information), confirming the stress-buffering role by the multihierarchy structure. Whereas, the Fe-Co-Ni NCs went through structural fracture after 1000 cycles (Figure S15d, Supporting Information), endowing that the lithium diffusion-induced mechanical stress toward Fe-Co-Ni NCs solid bulk results in severe volume expansion and electrode pulverization as cycling proceeds. As schematically illustrated in Figure 1b, the superior structural integrity of NFSs can be credited with the multistratal strain-buffering strategies in what follows: 1) the abundant omnibearing strain-release points originated from the ultrafine subunits can evenly release the mechanical strain; 2) the hollow architecture facilitates abundant surfaces toward radial expansion; and 3) the open-frame structure effectively eases asymmetrical tangential stress
upon long-term (de)lithiation.[26] These features confer the accommodated volume variation and outstanding electrochemical performance of Fe-Co-Ni NFSs.
To theoretically expound the superiority of pre-eminent electrochemical capability of the trimetallic Fe-Co-Ni MOFs, the electronic structures and corresponding lithium storage performance of Fe-Ni MOFs, Co-Ni MOFs, and Fe-Co-Ni MOFs were investigated by density functional theory (DFT) calculations (Figure S16, Supporting Information). Figure 6a illustrates the calculated total density of state (TDOS) profiles bound up with the Fermi level \(\left(\mathrm{E_{f}}\right)\) , indicating that the coordination modes of cyano ligand are the prerequisites for the 3d orbital electron spin of Fe, Co, and Ni according to their field strength. Compared with that of Fe-Ni MOFs and Co-Ni MOFs, the metallicity of Fe-Co-Ni MOFs features the dense and continuous Fermi levels throughout the conduction bands, manifesting the boosted framework conductivity induced by the multi-component metallic species. According to the partial density of state (PDOS) in Figure 6b, it is noteworthy that new electronic states of 3d orbital for low-spin C-coordinated Fe and Co and high-spin C-coordinated Ni are observed, which is relative to the occupied empty states, indicating that the electronic structure is manipulated by coordination environment in Fe-Co-Ni MOFs.
The lithium storage was systematically calculated to elucidate the stability of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) occupation at possible interstitial positions. Based on symmetry properties, nine possible \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) interstices in Fe-Co-Ni MOFs are considered, including body-centered (8c), face-centered between low-spin C-coordinated Fe and high-spin N-coordinated Ni (24d (Fe-Ni)), face-centered between low-spin C-coordinated Fe/Co and high-spin N-coordinated Ni (24d (Fe-Ni-Co)), face-centered between low-spin C-coordinated Co and high-spin N-coordinated Ni (24d (Co-Ni)), displaced between 8c and 24d (48 g (Fe-Ni) and 48 g (Co-Ni)), displaced from 8c positions toward low-spin C-coordinated Co corner (32c (Co)), displaced from 8c positions toward low-spin C-coordinated Fe corner (32c (Fe)), and displaced from 8c positions toward high-spin N-coordinated Ni corner (32n (Ni)) with Wyckoff notations.[56,57] Among which, the 8c site possesses the largest interstitial space whereas the 24d site provides the smallest space. Upon optimization, \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) was initially positioned at 48 g (Fe-Ni) and 32c (Fe) positions spontaneously transfer to 24d (Fe-Ni), and the ones at 8c and 48 g (Co-Ni) locations spontaneously diffuse to 24d (Co-Ni) site, suggesting \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) position at 24d is prominently more stable. As for Fe-Co-Ni MOFs (Figure 6c–g), \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) preferably occupies the 24d (Co-Ni) interstitial site with a lower binding energy than that of the 24d (Fe-Ni), 24d (Fe-Ni-Co), 32c (Co), and 32n (Ni) occupancies. In terms of Fe-Ni MOFs and Co-Ni MOFs, the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) was initially located at 48 g site and them ultimately diffused to 24d site, and there are four stable interstitial positions (Figures S17 and S18, Supporting Information). Meanwhile, the binding energies of Fe-Ni MOFs and Co-Ni MOFs are dramatically higher than that of Fe-Co-Ni MOFs, as summarized in Table S2 (Supporting Information). These results affirm that the Fe-Co-Ni MOFs is more energetically preferable for \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) insertion, which is paramount for more active sites and exalted electrochemical kinetics toward superior lithium storage performance. Furthermore, the 2D heatmap and 3D differential charge density distributions of Fe-Co-Ni MOFs before and after \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) lithiation are illustrated in Figure 6h and i. Similar to Fe-Ni MOFs and Co-Ni MOFs (Figures S19 and S20, Supporting Information), the charge diverts strikingly around Fe and Co, but slightly around Ni, demonstrating that electrochemical
mechanism dominantly depends on redox processes of Fe, Co, and Ni, which coincides with the experimental kinetic analysis. Ascribed to compositional diversity, the Fe-Co-Ni MOFs coinstantaneously achieves the synergistic effect due to the multi-metallic component at the different working voltage ranges and thus exalts the superior lithium storage performance.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we have synthesized trimetallic Fe-Co-Ni MOFs with nanoframe superstructures architecture as a versatile anode material for lithium storage to exalt the suppressed electronic conductivity and inferior cycling stability. Expounded by the experimental and
theoretical investigations, the Fe-Co-Ni NFSs feature pronounced strain-buffering effect via intriguing multi-hierarchical architecture, boosted electronic conductivity due to multiple components, and favorable Li adsorption energy, further achieving expedited diffusion kinetics and multiple active sites, which are the prerequisites for extraordinary lithium storage performance. Expectedly, the Fe-Co-Ni NFSs achieves distinguished electrochemical capability integrated with exceptional specific capacity \( (1030\,\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}} \) at \( 0.1\,\mathrm{A\,g^{-1}} \) ), fabulous rate capacity \( (414\,\mathrm{\mAh\,g^{-1}} \) at \( 2\,\mathrm{A\,g^{-1}} \) ), and superior cyclability \( (489\,\mathrm{\mAh\,g^{-1}} \) upon 1000 loops at \( 1\,\mathrm{A\,g^{-1}} \) ).
## 4. Experimental Section
Synthesis of Trimetallic Fe-Co-Ni nanocubes (NCs): In a typical synthetic protocol, trimetallic Fe-Co-Ni NCs were synthesized by the solution coprecipitation method. Firstly, \( 1.1\,\mathrm{g} \) of sodium citrate, \( 3\,\mathrm{g} \) of polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP, K30, \( M_{\mathrm{w}}=40\,000 \) ), and \( 0.7\,\mathrm{g} \) of nickel nitrate were dissolved in \( 40\,\mathrm{mL} \) of deionized (DI) water to form a uniform solution A. Afterward, \( 0.26\,\mathrm{g} \) of potassium hexacyanocobaltate (III) and \( 0.26\,\mathrm{g} \) of potassium hexacyanoferrate (III) was distributed in \( 40\,\mathrm{mL} \) of DI water to form a uniform solution B. The solution B was quickly poured into the solution A under vigorously magnetic stirring for \( 24\,\mathrm{h} \) at ambient temperature. The resulting yellow precipitates were centrifuged and washed with DI water for three times, and subsequently dried in vacuum oven at \( 80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) overnight.
Synthesis of Trimetallic Fe-Co-Ni nanoboxes (NBs), nanoframes (NFs), and nanoframe superstructures (NFSs): \( 103\,\mathrm{g} \) of the above prepared Fe-Co-Ni nanocubes was dispersed in a \( 60\,\mathrm{mL} \) solution (containing \( 20\,\mathrm{mL} \) of ammonia, \( 20\,\mathrm{mL} \) DI water and \( 20\,\mathrm{mL} \) ethanol) by ultrasonication to form a homogeneous suspension followed by continuous mechanical stirring for another \( 30\,\mathrm{min} \). The above suspension was then sealed in \( 150\,\mathrm{mL} \) Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclaves and hydrothermally treated at \( 100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for different set durations \( (6\,\mathrm{h} \) for Fe-Co-Ni NBs, \( 12\,\mathrm{h} \) for Fe-Co-Ni NFs, and \( 48\,\mathrm{h} \) for Fe-Co-Ni NFSs). After cooling down to ambient temperature naturally, then, the precipitates were centrifuged and washed with DI water for three times, and dried in vacuum for further use.
Morphological and structural characterization: The crystallinity and phase information were observed by X-ray diffraction (XRD, Ultima IV, Japan) using Cu \( \mathsf{K}_{\mathsf{a}} \) radiation with the voltage of \( 50\,\mathrm{kV} \) and the current of \( 30\,\mathrm{mA} \) within the \( 2\theta \) range of \( 10{-}90^{\circ} \). The thermal stability was identified by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA, NETZSCH TG 209 F1 \( 16\,\mathrm{C} \), Germany) from atmospheric temperature to \( 800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) with a heating rate of \( 5\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\,\mathrm{min}^{-1} \) under a nitrogen flow. The specific surface area and pore-size distribution were characterized by \( \mathsf{N}_{2} \) adsorption/desorption isotherms at \( 77\,\mathrm{k} \) using Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET, Micromeritics ASAP 2010, USA). The chemical composition of all the samples was collected by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Thermo Scientific \( K\!\cdot\!\mathsf{A l p h a}^{+} \), USA) using Al \( \mathsf{K}\alpha \) radiation and Raman spectra (Renishaw inVia, UK) with an argon ion laser beam. The surface morphology and structure information of the samples were measured by field emission scanning electron microscopy (SEM, FEI Quanta 250 FEG, USA), transmission electron microscopy (TEM, FEI Talos F200X, USA) with high-angle annular dark-field (HAADF- STEM) and energy dispersive X-ray spectrometer (EDS).
Electrochemical measurements: The electrochemical property analysis was investigated by 2032-type coin cells, which the battery assembly was performed in an Ar-filled glovebox (Mikrouna, Germany, \( \mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\leq0.1 \) ppm, \( \mathsf{O}_{2}\leq0.1\ \mathsf{p p m} \) ) with Celgard 2400 membrane served as separator, 1: 1: 1 organic solvent mixture by volume of ethylene carbonate, diethyl carbonate, and ethyl methyl carbonate (EC : DEC : EMC) with \( 1\,\mathsf{M}\,\mathsf{L}\mathsf{i}\mathsf{P}\mathsf{F}_{6} \) as electrolyte, and lithium foil as the counter electrode. The as-prepared materials were kept in vacuum oven for overnight at \( 100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) before making electrode slurry. For the half-cell testing, the working electrode was prepared by thoroughly mixing the \( 80\,\mathrm{\wt.\%} \) as-synthesized material, \( 10\,\mathrm{\wt.\%} \) polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF), and \( 10\,\mathrm{\wt.\%} \) acetylene black (Super P) in \( N \)-methylpyrrolidone (NMP) with vigorous stirring for \( 12\,\mathrm{h} \), which then was coated on copper foil and vacuum dried at \( 100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 12\,\mathrm{h} \). Furthermore, the galvanostatic charge/discharge cycling test in the voltage window of \( 0.01{-}3\,\mathrm{V} \) was
conducted on Land Test System (Land CT 3001A, China) at various current densities. The cyclic voltammetry (CV) and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were performed on an electrochemical workstation (CHI-760E, China) at the voltage amplitude of \( 5\,\mathrm{mV} \) with frequency from \( 10^{5}\,\mathrm{Hz} \) to \( 10^{-2}\,\mathrm{Hz} \). The full-cell was coupled by Fe-Co-Ni NFSs as anode and commercial Prussian blue (PB, Shanghai Macklin Biochemical Co., Ltd) as cathode using the same separator and electrolyte. The PB cathode was prepared by mixing the \( 80\,\mathrm{\wt.\%} \) PB, \( 10\,\mathrm{\wt.\%} \) PVDF, and \( 10\,\mathrm{\wt.\%} \) Super P of NMP with vigorous stirring for \( 12\,\mathrm{h} \), which then was coated on aluminum foil and vacuum dried at \( 100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 12\,\mathrm{h} \). The theoretical specific capacity of Fe-Co-Ni MOF was calculated by the following law: \( C_{\mathrm{Fe-Co-Ni}} \) \( \mathsf{M O F}=(n F) \) / \( (M_{\mathsf{F e-C o-N i}}\,\mathsf{M O F}) \), where \( n \) is the adsorbed Li atoms quantity. \( F \) is the Faraday constant, and \( M_{\mathsf{F e-C o-N i}}\,\mathsf{M O F} \) is the molar weight of the FeCo-Ni MOF. Herein, the theoretical specific capacity is calculated to be \( 430.6\,\mathrm{mAh\,g^{-1}} \) for Fe-Co-Ni NCs \( (\mathsf{N i}_{3}[\mathsf{F e}_{0.5}\mathsf{C o}_{0.53}(\mathsf{C N})_{6}]_{2}\cdot7.73\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}) \), \( 437.2\,\mathrm{mAh\,g^{-1}} \) for Fe-Co-Ni NBs \( (\mathsf{N i}_{3}[\mathsf{F e}_{0.54}\mathsf{C o}_{0.49}(\mathsf{C N})_{6}]_{2}\cdot7.18\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}) \), \( 448.2\,\mathrm{mAh\,g^{-1}} \) for Fe-Co-Ni NFs \( (\mathsf{N i}_{3}[\mathsf{F e}_{0.48}\mathsf{C o}_{0.52}(\mathsf{C N})_{6}]_{2}\cdot6.35\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}) \), and \( 448.2\,\mathrm{mAh\,g^{-1}} \) for Fe-Co-Ni NFSs \( (\mathsf{N i}_{3}[\mathsf{F e}_{0.51}\mathsf{C o}_{0.46}(\mathsf{C N})_{6}]_{2}\cdot6.56\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}) \).
